      "text": "First, we need to determine the side length of the square. Since the diagonal of a square is \\( \\sqrt{2} \\) times the side length, we can set up the equation \\( \\sqrt{2} \\cdot s = 5 \\), where \\( s \\) is the side length of the square. Solving for \\( s \\), we get \\( s = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\).\n\nThe area of the square is then \\( s^2 = \\left( \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{25 \\cdot 2}{4} = \\frac{50}{4} = 12.5 \\) square inches.\n\nNext, we need to determine the radius of the circle. Since the diameter of the circle is 5 inches, the radius is \\( \\frac{5}{2} = 2.5 \\) inches.\n\nThe area of the circle is given by the formula \\( \\pi r^2 \\). Substituting the radius, we get the area as \\( \\pi (2.5)^2 = \\pi \\cdot 6.25 = 6.25\\pi \\) square inches.\n\nTo find the difference in area between the circle and the square, we subtract the area of the square from the area of the circle:\n\\[ 6.25\\pi - 12.5 \\]\n\nUsing \\( \\pi \\approx 3.14159 \\), we get:\n\\[ 6.25 \\cdot 3.14159 - 12.5 \\approx 19.63495625 - 12.5 = 7.13495625 \\]\n\nRounding to the nearest tenth, the difference in area is:\n\\[ \\boxed{7.1} \\]",
