      "text": "To determine by how many square inches the area of the circle is greater than the area of the square, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Find the side length of the square:**\n   The diagonal of the square is given as 5 inches. Let the side length of the square be \\( s \\). The relationship between the side length and the diagonal of a square is given by the Pythagorean theorem:\n   \\[\n   s\\sqrt{2} = 5\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( s \\):\n   \\[\n   s = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the area of the square:**\n   The area \\( A_{\\text{square}} \\) of the square is:\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{square}} = s^2 = \\left( \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\right)^2 = \\frac{25 \\cdot 2}{4} = \\frac{50}{4} = 12.5 \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Find the radius of the circle:**\n   The diameter of the circle is given as 5 inches. The radius \\( r \\) of the circle is:\n   \\[\n   r = \\frac{5}{2} = 2.5 \\text{ inches}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the area of the circle:**\n   The area \\( A_{\\text{circle}} \\) of the circle is:\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{circle}} = \\pi r^2 = \\pi (2.5)^2 = \\pi \\cdot 6.25 = 6.25\\pi \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n   Using the approximation \\(\\pi \\approx 3.14\\):\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{circle}} \\approx 6.25 \\times 3.14 = 19.625 \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Find the difference in areas:**\n   The difference in the areas between the circle and the square is:\n   \\[\n   A_{\\text{circle}} - A_{\\text{square}} \\approx 19.625 - 12.5 = 7.125 \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Round the final answer to the nearest tenth:**\n   \\[\n   7.125 \\approx 7.1\n   \\]\n\nThus, the area of the circle is greater than the area of the square by \\(\\boxed{7.1}\\) square inches.",
