      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "First, we need to find the side length of the square. Since the diagonal of the square is 5 inches, we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the side length. Let $s$ be the side length of the square. Then, we have:\n$$\ns^2 + s^2 = 5^2 \\Rightarrow 2s^2 = 25 \\Rightarrow s^2 = \\frac{25}{2} \\Rightarrow s = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNext, we find the area of the square:\n$$\n\\text{Area of the square} = s^2 = \\left(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{25 \\times 2}{4} = \\frac{50}{4} = 12.5 \\text{ square inches}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the area of the circle. Since the diameter of the circle is 5 inches, the radius $r$ is:\n$$\nr = \\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the circle is given by the formula:\n$$\n\\text{Area of the circle} = \\pi r^2 = \\pi \\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)^2 = \\pi \\times \\frac{25}{4} = \\frac{25\\pi}{4} \\approx 19.634954084936208 \\text{ square inches}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the difference between the area of the circle and the area of the square:\n$$\n\\text{Difference} = \\frac{25\\pi}{4} - 12.5 \\approx 19.634954084936208 - 12.5 = 7.134954084936207 \\approx \\boxed{7.1}.\n$$\n\nThe final answer is \\boxed{7.1} square inches.",
